<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Lingen</h1><p class="st-meta">1493 CE – 1702 CE (approx.) · capital Lingen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small county on the Ems that passed from the counts of Tecklenburg to the Habsburgs, the house of Orange and finally Prussia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Lingen on the Ems belonged to the lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tecklenburg">County of Tecklenburg</a> until a division of the family's territory in the late 15th century made it a separate county under a junior line. Little is known of its earliest years as a separate county.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Count Konrad of Tecklenburg-Lingen joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a> and introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>; after its defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Charles V</a> confiscated Lingen in 1548 and gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-of-egmond">Maximilian of Egmond-Buren</a>, whose heiress married William of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-orange">Orange</a>. Lingen then changed hands between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the house of Orange during the Eighty Years' War, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">Oranges</a> founded a Calvinist academy there in 1697.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-england">William III of England</a>, also prince of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-orange">Orange</a>, died in 1702, Lingen passed to his Prussian heir Frederick I and became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>. It belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> from 1815 to 1866 and is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-lingen.jpg" alt="County of Lingen" width="320" height="480"><figcaption>Image: Michielverbeek · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lingen,_die_evangelisch-reformierte_Kirche_gespiegeld_in_de_ramen_van_die_Kornblume_IMG_7118_2020-08-06_14.08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1493 CE – 1702 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lingen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Konrad of Tecklenburg, William III of Orange</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, Calvinism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lingen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1598&amp;focus=county-of-lingen" aria-label="County of Lingen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4844 366 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Lingen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5164 622L5229 694M5164 622L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5164 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5164 622h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1598&amp;focus=county-of-lingen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
